WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:04.800
I'm in London to
meet Joshua Coombes.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.400
He's a hairdresser.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.266
And he believes small acts of
love can make a big impact.

00:00:13.834 --> 00:00:15.867
Joshua hopes he can
help the homeless,

00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:19.667
not by offering
them money or food

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.100
but by giving them a haircut.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.900
The reason I started cutting
hair on the street, initially,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.800
was actually the
magnitude of the problem,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.333
like seeing how big this
problem is in each city.

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:31.400
It's really hard to know
what to do about that.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.533
And I think it was from that
feeling I had to impact today.

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:36.800
I had to do something positive
to make someone smile.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.200
And realize that
even if you can't

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.200
solve the problem, actually it's
OK to just do your own thing.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.533
And for me, that's hairdressing.

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:45.567
I love what I do, so it's a
great way to be able to give

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.400
someone confidence.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.100
When was the last time
you had a haircut?

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.200
In November last year.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.367
So January, February.

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.800
Five months to March, April.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.934
How did you come to
be homeless, Stuart?

00:00:56.934 --> 00:01:00.633
I broke my hip and
they stopped my benefits.

00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:03.633
At the time, I was
like, that's stupid.

00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:05.467
And involved with
drugs and things.

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:06.467
I didn't really care.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:09.767
And next thing, [inaudible].

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:13.667
That was 24th of January, 2016.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:17.200
And I've been homeless since.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.900
You see lots of things
with homelessness.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.133
And there's lots of stereotypes.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.967
And really, what I do is
try and humanize this issue.

00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:25.633
Because as I said,
this problem is so big,

00:01:25.633 --> 00:01:27.133
I think when you bring
it down to one person

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:29.433
and tell the story, that's
a great way to start

00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:33.333
and a great way for
people to understand.

00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:35.200
For me, like one of
the most amazing moments

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.467
is giving someone the mirror
at the end of their haircut

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.867
because it's great to
see someone's reaction

00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:41.533
because they might
recognize someone they

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:44.900
haven't seen for a few years.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.934
5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:51.633
Ta-da.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.300
I'm 20 years younger.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.100
[inaudible]

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.533
Nobody's going
to recognize me.

00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:04.200
Mate, you're a
new man right now.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.000
Yeah, thank you.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.567
How do you feel?

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:08.367
Like a new man.

00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:09.467
Like a new man.

00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:12.200
Do you recognize yourself there?

